      "id": 1,
      "text": "Colbert got its name in 1902 from Henry Colbert, a sawmill owner who also became the town’s first postmaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/colbert_washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the early 1900s Colbert boasted five sawmills, two saloons, three livery stables, two blacksmith shops, and several general stores.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/colbert_washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "At 1,841 feet above sea level, Colbert experiences a humid continental climate with over 43 inches of snow each winter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        23,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/colbert_washington",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mount Spokane lies about 15 miles northeast, and the nearby Selkirk Mountains add steeper slopes and coniferous forests that shape Colbert’s microclimate.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/colbert_washington",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before settlers arrived, the area was part of the traditional territory of the Spokane Tribe, who fished, hunted, and gathered along the Little Spokane River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/colbert_washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Little Spokane River runs just north of Colbert, offering fishing, kayaking, and scenic views that locals enjoy year-round.",
